language variant
language en-gb-x-rp
name fairydust
gender female 18

// CLASSIC, PRESSED FOUR TIMES — fairydust.
//
// Taken from the jaimla template and lifted: same base (en-gb-x-rp), same idea
// that restraint is what makes a voice alluring, carried up an octave and made
// lighter. Where jaimla is close and warm, this is high and weightless — and it
// is the only voice in the cast with no authority in it at all, which is the
// point of putting it at the end of a row of machines that have nothing else.

// AN OCTAVE ABOVE JAIMLA, AND STILL A FIFTH WIDE.
// jaimla spans F3-C4 (175-262), a perfect fifth, because the narrowness is the
// intimacy. This keeps the interval and moves the whole thing up: 260-390 is
// C4-G4, the same 3:2 ratio an octave higher. Keeping the RATIO rather than the
// span is what makes this read as the same voice grown light rather than as a
// different one squeaking — an octave up with a wider interval would be shrill,
// and shrill is the failure mode this voice is one step away from at all times.
pitch 260 390

// A SHORT, BRIGHT TRACT — AND NO CHEST WHATSOEVER.
// jaimla lifts F1-F3 and keeps the top soft. This goes further on both counts:
// the formants are higher again, and F0 is pulled DOWN hard (100 -> 78 at the
// low end) because weight is the one thing this voice must not have. What is
// left is all upper-mid — the register a voice occupies when it is not pushing
// air, only shaping it.
formant 0  78 104  92
formant 1 118 132 116
formant 2 128 134 110
formant 3 126 128  96
formant 4 120 118  80
formant 5 112 108  64
formant 6 102  96  44
formant 7  94  88  32
formant 8  88  82  26

// AIR WITH A SHIMMER ON IT. The peak moves up to 1.6 kHz and there is a
// deliberate lift at 4.2 kHz that no other voice here has — that band is where
// breath turns to sparkle, and it is the whole reason the voice reads as
// dust rather than as a small person. Below 400 Hz there is almost nothing.
tone 400 105 1600 235 2800 200 4200 165 6000 90

// BREATH IS MOST OF IT.
// jaimla is voicing 62 with breath 2-5. This is voicing 44 with breath wider
// still: less than half the signal is fold vibration, and the rest is moving
// air. That is why it is soft at any volume — there is very little there to be
// loud with, and the loudness normaliser cannot manufacture a body that was
// never in the recording.
breath   5 7 8 8 8 8 7 6
breathw 130 190 215 215 215 215 190 150
voicing 44
roughness 0
flutter 2

// The lightest consonants in the cast — softer even than jaimla's 58/46. Nothing
// is struck. The 2.8 kHz shelf in the tone curve is what keeps the words
// legible, because the consonants themselves are barely doing it.
consonants 50 40

// A long, quiet shimmer rather than a room: it should sound like it is still
// there slightly after it has stopped.
echo 190 7

// IT RISES. jaimla keeps the full melody because the pleasure of that voice is
// that it moves; this moves more. stressAdd climbs further than any other voice
// here and stressAmp stays almost flat, so emphasis is entirely a lift and never
// a push. Nothing in this voice ever gets louder to be heard.
intonation 1
stressAmp   12 12 13 13 14 14 15 15
stressAdd    0 14 32 52 74 96 130 165
